= Red Lentils (Masūra)

_Ayurvedic status: fully classical — masūra is one of the principal pulses named in the saṃhitās, treated as the canonical Pitta-pacifying lentil and the convalescent-diet legume complement to mudga. Chinese: not classical — lentils were known in classical China only marginally; 兵豆 (bīngdòu, "soldier's bean") and 扁豆 (biǎndòu, lablab; distinct species) are sometimes confused with lentils in modern Chinese translations. Greco-Roman: fully classical — phakē is one of the principal Greek pulses, treated extensively by Dioscorides and Hippocrates; "lentil" survives as one of the canonical Mediterranean staples. The genus_ Lens _gave its name to the optical lens, after the biconvex seed shape._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Lens culinaris_ Medik., family Fabaceae (Leguminosae); older synonym _Lens esculenta_ Moench. The drug-foodstuff is the *cotyledon* (the dehulled split form, which is the principal culinary form). The *red / orange* lentil (Hindi _masūr dāl_, the dehulled split _Lens culinaris_) is what cooks pink-orange and disintegrates on cooking; the unhulled whole form is brown-skinned. Other lentil categories — *green Puy lentil* (var. _vulgaris_), *Beluga black lentil* — are different cultivars with whole-seed coats retained. _Lens_ is the Classical Latin for the lentil (Pliny _N.H._ XVIII.31) and is the source via metaphor of the optical _lens_ (the biconvex shape of the polished glass naming itself after the seed); _culinaris_ = "of the kitchen."#footnote[Etymology: Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _lens_, _culinaris_); the optical-lens metaphor from the seed shape is a standard etymology — _Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "lens, n."). Species identity: standard botanical authority.]

*English:* _Lentil_, from Old French _lentille_, from Latin _lenticula_ (diminutive of _lens_), "little lens." _Red lentil_ specifies the dehulled, orange-pink cotyledon form (also _Egyptian lentil_, _split red lentil_, Hindi-influenced English _masoor dal_).#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "lentil, n."); Hindi-influenced _masoor dal_ is standard Indian-English.]

*Chinese:* No classical entry under a single canonical name. Modern Chinese: 扁豆 (biǎndòu) is *lablab bean*, a distinct species (_Lablab purpureus_); *扁豆* applied to lentil is a translator's confusion. The modern descriptive 兵豆 (bīngdòu, "soldier's bean") and 小扁豆 (xiǎo biǎndòu, "small lablab") and 紅兵豆 (hóng bīngdòu, "red soldier's bean") are used; none is classical Bencao.

*Sanskrit:* मसूर (_masūra_) — the canonical name; etymology obscure (possibly from a Dravidian or Munda substrate, like other classical Indian pulse names). Synonyms: मङ्गल्या (_maṅgalyā_, "auspicious"), रक्तमसूर (_rakta-masūra_, "red masūra" specifically), पीतमसूर (_pīta-masūra_, "yellow masūra"). The dehusked-split form is *मसूरदाल* (_masūra-dāla_) in later Sanskrit; classically just _masūra_ with preparation specified.#footnote[Monier-Williams, _A Sanskrit–English Dictionary_ (s.v. _masūra_, _maṅgalyā_); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_, _masūra_ entry.]

*Greek:* φακή (_phakḗ_) — the canonical Greek name; *Esau's lentil-stew* in the Septuagint translation of Genesis 25:29–34 is φακὸς (_phakòs_) — the famous "mess of pottage" / *ἕψημα φακοῦ* (_hépsēma phakoû_, "lentil-pottage") for which Esau sold his birthright. The Hebrew _ʿadāšîm_ (עֲדָשִׁים) is the original.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_No classical Chinese entry for masūra; placement on Chinese-medical axes is reasoned cross-system._

By reasoned placement: sweet (甘), slightly cool to neutral (微涼/平); Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Heart (心); reasoned actions 健脾 (strengthens Spleen), 補血 (blood-supportive — modern, by analogy with the iron content), 利水 (mild diuretic), 益氣 (qi-supportive). Pacifies heat-pattern dyspepsia; less appropriate for vacuity-cold stools.#footnote[Reasoned placement; not classical Chinese attribution. The iron-content / 補血 reasoning is interpretive.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

Masūra is canonical — one of the "best of pulses" alongside mudga, with a Pitta-pacifying register that contrasts cleanly with mudga's broader tridoṣa applicability.#footnote[Masūra in Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27 (_annapānavidhi_, _śimbi-varga_); Suśruta Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 46; Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_, _masūra_ entry — Chunekar & Pandey (Chaukhamba). Translations: Sharma & Dash; Murthy.]

*Rasa (taste):* Madhura (मधुर, sweet), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent).

*Guṇa (qualities):* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry).

*Vīrya (potency):* Śīta (शीत, cooling).

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).#footnote[Rasa–guṇa–vīrya–vipāka of masūra: Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_; standard dravyaguna.]

*Prabhāva:* No distinct prabhāva is classically named for masūra; actions flow from the sweet-astringent-light-dry-cool-pungent-vipāka profile.

*Doṣa effect:* Pacifies Pitta and Kapha; aggravates Vāta (the laghu-rūkṣa, more strongly so than mudga, which is more tridoṣa-balanced). Masūra is the *cooler and drier* pulse; requires more ghee-and-spice correction than mudga in classical preparation.

*Karma:* _Grāhī_ (binding stools — characteristic, more strongly than mudga), _hṛdya_, _rakta-prasādana_ (blood-clarifying — the red-color → blood-supportive folk-medical reading; consistent with iron content), _balya_ (modest), _bṛṃhaṇa_ (in moderation; if heavy, can cause Vāta). Used in: _atisāra_ (diarrhea — the grāhī action), _rakta-pitta_ (bleeding-from-heat) — distinctive Pitta-blood register; convalescent diet; gentle pulse for fever-recovery.#footnote[Karma compiled from Caraka Saṃhitā, Sūtrasthāna 27, and Cikitsāsthāna chapters on _atisāra_ (19) and _raktapitta_ (4); Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Dhānya-varga_.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S/N) Conditional:* Vāta excess (the laghu-rūkṣa); requires unctuous preparation (ghee + asafoetida + cumin / ginger).
- *(N) Saṃyoga viruddha:* heavy masūra combinations with sour curd or heavy meat in some regional nighaṇṭus.
- *(M) Modern:* mild oligosaccharide flatulence (similar to other pulses); lentil-allergy uncommon; phytic-acid mineral-binding (reduced by soaking and proper cooking).

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

_Lentils are canonical in Tibb under the name_ ʿadas _— one of the foundational Mediterranean-Levantine pulse staples. See_ tibb-al-arabi.typ.

*Arabic:* عدس (_ʿadas_) — the canonical name; the same Semitic root as Hebrew _ʿadāšîm_ (the Esau's pottage word). Persian: عدس (_ʿadas_); also مسور (_masūr_, from Sanskrit). Urdu/Hakeemi: مسور (_masūr_).

*Mizāj:* *Cold and Dry in the 2nd degree* (Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _ʿadas_). Converges with the Ayurvedic śīta-rūkṣa profile and the Greco-Roman cooling-drying reading — clean tri-system convergence.

*Af'āl:* *Mughadhdhī yasīr* (lightly nourishing); *muqawwī al-mi'da*; *qābiḍ* (binding — the canonical anti-diarrheal action, paralleling the Ayurvedic _grāhī_ and the Dioscoridean "binds the bowels"); *muḥaffif li-l-ṣafrā'* (refines bile); *mughayyir li-l-balgham* (modifies phlegm); the *mughallaẓ li-l-dam* (thickens blood) classical attribution underlies the Tibb caution against heavy lentil consumption for the melancholic constitution.

*Istemālāt:* mild diarrhea (the binding action); chronic gastric weakness; convalescence (in moderation); thirst; hot-pattern fever with thirst.

*Mawāne':* melancholic constitution in excess (the classical Tibb caution); Vāta-equivalent dryness; severe constipation; psoriasis-like skin conditions (some Tibb texts).

*Muṣliḥ:* olive oil; vinegar; cumin; chard or sour pomegranate (the canonical Levantine *ʿadas bi-l-ḥāmiḍ*, "lentils with sour" preparation, observes the muṣliḥ rule); ghee for the Indian Unani register.

*Badal:* mung bean (*māsh*); chickpea (*ḥimmiṣ*) for general protein.

*Tibb formulas:* *Ḥasā' al-ʿAdas* (lentil broth, the canonical fever / convalescent food); *Mujaddara* (lentils + rice + onion, the Levantine classical dish — Tibb-aligned by virtue of its rice-pulse complementarity and the cumin / olive-oil muṣliḥ pairings); the famous Egyptian *koshary* lineage descends from this register. *ʿAdas yāyilā* / *ʿAdas Ḥalabī* — Aleppine regional preparations.

*Source attestation:* Ibn Sīnā, *al-Qānūn* II, _ʿadas_; Ibn al-Bayṭār, *al-Jāmiʿ*; *Khazāyin al-Adwiya*; the Tibb literature is built on the Galenic _phakē_ tradition.#footnote[Tibb ʿadas: classical Galenic-Avicennan reading, with continuous Mediterranean-Persian-Indian dietetic tradition; the cross-system convergence with Ayurveda and Greco-Roman pharmacology is exact.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formulas.

=== Ayurveda

- *Masūra yūṣa* — split-red-lentil soup with ghee, cumin, ginger, asafoetida; the Pitta-Kapha-pacifying convalescent soup, parallel to mudga-yūṣa in the convalescent dietary hierarchy.
- *Masūra-with-rice* (_masūra-bhāta_) — the typical South-Indian / North-Indian everyday combination; cereal-and-pulse complementarity.
- *Masūra ūpama* (modern South Indian) and various regional preparations.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

A substantial Greco-Roman tradition.

- *Greco-Roman:* _Dioscorides_ (_De Materia Medica_ II.107, _phakē_) treats lentils as nourishing but slow-digesting, binding the bowels (a clean cross-system convergence with the Ayurvedic _grāhī_ attribution), occasionally with side-effects in those with sensitive constitutions. _Pliny_ (_N.H._ XVIII.31, XXII.70) gives a long Latin account.
- *Roman cookery:* lentils were a staple Republican food (with emmer); the _puls fabata_ and _lens_ dishes are documented in Apicius.
- *Esau's pottage (Septuagint Genesis 25:29–34):* the famous "mess of pottage" sold for the birthright — a single phrase that has carried lentils into Western literary memory through three millennia.
- *Medieval European:* Hildegard von Bingen (_Physica_, late 12th c.) treats lentils with characteristic mixed-praise — nourishing but somewhat cooling and Vāta-equivalent in her humoral system; for the strong.
- *Modern Western:* lentils persist as a Mediterranean and Indian-cuisine staple; modern nutrition has rehabilitated them as a high-protein, low-glycemic-index, high-fiber pulse.#footnote[Greco-Roman: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ II.107 (Beck); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XVIII, XXII (Loeb); Apicius, _De Re Coquinaria_. Esau's pottage: Septuagint Gen. 25:29–34, the Hebrew _ʿadāšîm_, the standard Bible-reference. Hildegard: _Physica_ (Throop).]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Composition, dehulled split red lentils, raw, approximate per 100 g — verify against USDA FoodData Central.

- Energy ~350 kcal; Water ~8 g
- Protein ~25 g (high, with moderate lysine and modest methionine; complemented by cereals)
- Fat ~1 g
- Carbohydrate ~60 g; Dietary fiber ~10 g (dehulled split form is lower in fiber than whole)
- Folate ~480 µg (high)
- Iron ~7 mg (non-heme; vitamin-C co-ingestion improves absorption)
- Magnesium ~120 mg; Potassium ~700 mg
- Phytic acid (reduced by soaking)

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Glycemic index*: low (~30) — favorable for type-2 diabetes / metabolic-syndrome contexts.
- *Cooks quickly* relative to other pulses (15–20 min); the dehulled split form needs no soaking.
- *Cereal-and-pulse complementarity*: the classical _kṛsara/khichari_ logic applies — combine with rice for complete amino-acid profile.
- *Phytohaemagglutinins / trypsin inhibitors:* present but low; destroyed by ordinary cooking.
- *Iron + vitamin C combination:* lentils + a squeeze of lime / amla / tomato substantially increases iron bioavailability (the classical Indian preparation logic).
- *Cardiovascular / metabolic:* observational data and small RCTs support lentil-as-part-of-Mediterranean-pattern cardiovascular benefits.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* No toxicological upper limit. Practical: routine cooked serving (~30–50 g raw / ~150–200 g cooked) is the dietary register; appropriate for daily / staple consumption. Risk groups: lentil allergy (uncommon); FODMAP-sensitive IBS (case-by-case — split dehulled form tolerates better than whole).

